    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Runny nose, sore throat and cough for three days. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Hippocrates asked for a same-day visit. Three days ago he had a scratchy throat, then a runny nose, sneezing and a mild dry cough; several of his pupils have had colds. He has felt warm but has not measured a fever. He is eating and drinking normally and has no shortness of breath, chest pain or earache. Two nights ago he took a nighttime cold remedy from the pharmacy; it contained diphenhydramine and a decongestant, and he woke at night groggy and unsteady on the way to the toilet, holding the rail. He did not take it again and brought the box to show. He has taken acetaminophen twice a day. He has not fallen. His knees are better on the gel and the exercises. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Sore throat, runny nose, sneezing, mild dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild; sleeping less well</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Colds among his pupils; one dose of a nighttime cold remedy with diphenhydramine made him unsteady</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Acetaminophen, warm drinks</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No shortness of breath, chest pain, earache or confusion</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

