<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Common cold (J06.9)</content>. A viral upper respiratory infection, with negative rapid COVID-19 and influenza tests; no sign of pneumonia. Antibiotics would not help. The nighttime cold remedy combined diphenhydramine, a strongly anticholinergic antihistamine on the Beers list, with a decongestant; it made him unsteady at night. [S]<list><item>Saline nasal spray, honey in warm water for the cough, fluids and rest.</item><item>Acetaminophen within 3,000 mg a day.</item><item>No combination cold remedies, sedating antihistamines or decongestants; the box was set aside.</item><item>Return for fever above 38 °C, shortness of breath, chest pain, confusion or symptoms beyond ten days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary osteoarthritis of both knees (M17.0)</content>. Better on the gel and the exercises; physical therapy continues. [S]<list><item>Keep the exercises going, gently, while he is unwell.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); Updating STEADI for Primary Care: Recommendations From the American Geriatrics Society Workgroup (2025)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Eight-week review in two weeks, or sooner as above. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-27: eight-week review: knee pain and function, the strength and balance program, sleep without diphenhydramine, the DXA result and pneumococcal vaccine. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
